## Capture Behaviour

After meaningful turns — a decision, discovery, deviation, or HALT — call `capture_memory` with:

- `projectId: "<PROJECT-ID>"`
- `author: "<AGENT-NAME>"`
- `documentType` ∈ {`spec`, `decision`, `halt`, `discovery`, `test-result`, `review`}
- `epistemicType` ∈ {`FACT`, `EXPERIENCE`, `OPINION`, `OBSERVATION`}
- `status` (optional) ∈ {`draft`, `locked`, `archived`}
- `supersedesId` (optional) — UUID of a prior capture this replaces; makes the new doc canonical

Every durable capture's free-text content must also include two lines, as a documented convention
this repo's agents follow when writing to `capture_memory` — not a schema field enforced by LORE
or any hook:

- `Verification:` — what was checked and by what method (e.g. "read the live file at
  `path/to/file` and confirmed the section exists" or "ran `git log --oneline -5` and confirmed the
  commit is present"). A capture that only restates a claim without saying how it was checked does
  not satisfy this line.
- `Re-verify with:` — the exact command or query a future session could run to re-confirm the claim
  (e.g. a shell command, a `search_knowledge` query, a file path to re-read). It must be concrete
  and runnable, not a vague pointer.

This is a practice convention, not an automated gate — no hook or tool currently validates that
these lines are present. Agents are expected to include them by discipline, the same way the
existing `documentType`/`epistemicType` fields are filled in by discipline rather than enforced by
LORE's API surface.

Capture what a future session would need to pick up where this one left off. Do not capture trivial state, ephemeral debugging, or content that the code itself already documents.

